# The 1.1.3 user path: CDE onto released 1.1.1 and 1.1.2 systems, as their users type it.
# Disks (UGATE_DISKS/<name>.hdf): the released images, installed and first-booted, nothing else done to them.
#   1.1.2-k30-x11  1.1.2-k46-x11   the 1.1.2 4g images (x11 set), first-booted in the Z3660 machine's 030 / 060 shape
#   1.1.1-k30-x11                  the 1.1.1 k30 4g image, first-booted the same way
#   1.1.2-k30-std  1.1.2-k46-std   1.1.2 from the netinstall CD, standard set (no X), root 575 MiB, swap 128 MiB
# The 68030 rows take the two steps apkg names (a newer apkg, then cde): cde's own dependency floors must bring
# every package it needs. The 68040/68060 rows take the three steps the 1.1.3 notes give, since the File Manager
# needs the newer kernel; the second boot is the power cycle that boots it.

row x11-k30 k30 1.1.2-k30-x11 session=cde
	apkg update
	apkg upgrade apkg
	apkg install cde

row x11-k46 k46 1.1.2-k46-x11 session=cde
	apkg update
	apkg upgrade apkg
	apkg upgrade
	apkg install cde

# a 1.1.1 user who skips the first step: the old apkg cannot read cde's floors and changes nothing
row old-k30 k30 1.1.1-k30-x11 session=cde
	apkg update
	=5 apkg install cde
	apkg upgrade apkg
	apkg install cde

# a 1.1.2 user who skips 'apkg upgrade apkg' and types a plain 'apkg upgrade': apkg 0.3.13 lays the newer apkg
# first and hands the rest of the upgrade to it, so the floors hold; on k46 the upgrade brings the new kernel
row plain-k30 k30 1.1.2-k30-x11 session=cde
	apkg update
	apkg upgrade
	apkg install cde

row plain-k46 k46 1.1.2-k46-x11 session=cde
	apkg update
	apkg upgrade
	apkg install cde

row std-k30 k30 1.1.2-k30-std session=cde
	apkg update
	apkg upgrade apkg
	apkg install cde

row std-k46 k46 1.1.2-k46-std session=cde
	apkg update
	apkg upgrade apkg
	apkg upgrade
	apkg install cde
